# Files that are consumed by Linux containers must be checked out with LF, on
# every platform.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites these to CRLF in
# the working tree. Docker then COPYs them into an image verbatim, and the
# shebang becomes `#!/bin/sh\r`. The kernel looks for an interpreter literally
# named "/bin/sh\r", does not find one, and the container dies with:
#
#   exec /usr/local/bin/squid-entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# naming a file that is plainly there and plainly executable. It cost a
# debugging session on the SWG stack, which builds fine on Linux and macOS and
# crash-loops on Windows.
# Deliberately narrow. YAML and JSON are read by parsers that do not care about
# CRLF, and adding them here renormalises unrelated files across the repo for no
# benefit. Only what a Linux kernel or shell has to interpret literally.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
Dockerfile* text eol=lf

# PowerShell stays CRLF: it is consumed by Windows, not by a container.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
